    <article class="docs-prose">
      <h1 id="stage-13-metadata-and-index" class="group scroll-mt-20">Stage 13: Metadata and index<a href="#stage-13-metadata-and-index" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h1>
      <p>Add author, date, audience, keyword, and index metadata to the topics. Place it in <code>&lt;prolog&gt;</code>, between the short description and the body. Add publication metadata to the map's <code>&lt;topicmeta&gt;</code>.</p>
      <p>DITA makes all of this metadata optional. To require fields, set a metadata policy that requires keywords on topics and creation dates on tasks. The metadata policy is a DogsBay XML feature, not part of DITA: the policy is stored with the project, and the DogsBay XML check enforces it.</p>
      <p>The examples show each topic type; expand the remaining examples to apply the same structure.</p>
      <p><strong>Optional module.</strong> See <a href="/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/learning-path">Choose a learning path</a> for the starting checkpoint and the next core lesson.</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> about 40 minutes. <strong>You need:</strong> stage 12 complete.</p>
      <p>Recorded output below is an example. File counts, paths, and stage numbers can differ. <a href="/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/run-the-gate">Check your work</a> to see the result for your own project.</p>
      <h2 id="step-1-the-prolog-of-a-concept" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 1: The prolog of a concept<a href="#step-1-the-prolog-of-a-concept" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><code>topics/what-is-audacity.dita</code> This one carries every element the stage uses.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/what-is-audacity.dita\n+++ b/topics/what-is-audacity.dita\n@@ -4,6 +4,29 @@\n <concept id=\"what-is-audacity\">\n   <title>What is <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/>?</title>\n   <shortdesc><keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> is a free, open-source audio editor and recorder for Windows, macOS and Linux.</shortdesc>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <copyright>\n+      <copyryear year=\"2026\"/>\n+      <copyrholder>DogsBay Ltd.</copyrholder>\n+    </copyright>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-10\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <audience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"novice\"/>\n+      <category>concept</category>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>Audacity</keyword>\n+        <keyword>audio editor</keyword>\n+        <keyword>open source</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>Audacity<indexterm>overview</indexterm></indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>audio editor<index-see>Audacity</index-see></indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+    <resourceid appname=\"audacity-help\" id=\"intro\"/>\n+    <data name=\"reading-time\" value=\"2 min\"/>\n+  </prolog>\n   <conbody>\n     <p><keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> records live audio, imports and exports the common audio formats, and edits sound with cut, copy, paste and a library of effects.\n     It is free to download and use, and it runs on Windows, macOS and Linux.</p>"} lang="diff" title="topics/what-is-audacity.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the order</strong> The children of <code>&lt;prolog&gt;</code> come in a fixed order: <code>&lt;author&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;source&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;publisher&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;copyright&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;critdates&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;permissions&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;metadata&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;resourceid&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;data&gt;</code>. Skip any, but do not reorder them. <code>&lt;resourceid&gt;</code> comes after <code>&lt;metadata&gt;</code>. If you put it inside <code>&lt;metadata&gt;</code>, the file does not validate. <code>&lt;data&gt;</code> is allowed in both places: at the end of <code>&lt;metadata&gt;</code>, or after <code>&lt;resourceid&gt;</code> as here.
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;author type="creator"&gt;</code> names who wrote the topic; <code>type</code> may also be <code>contributor</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;copyright&gt;</code> holds a <code>&lt;copyryear year="…"/&gt;</code> and a <code>&lt;copyrholder&gt;</code>. Only this topic carries one; the map's copyright covers the guide.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;critdates&gt;</code> holds <code>&lt;created date="…"/&gt;</code> and, in the topics that have been revised, <code>&lt;revised modified="…"/&gt;</code>. Dates are <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;metadata&gt;</code> groups the classification: <code>&lt;audience type="user" experiencelevel="novice"/&gt;</code>, a <code>&lt;category&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;keywords&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;keywords&gt;</code> holds <code>&lt;keyword&gt;</code>s, which DITA-OT's <code>html5</code> transform writes into a <code>&lt;meta name="keywords"&gt;</code> tag, and <code>&lt;indexterm&gt;</code>s, which the HTML pages do not show: a PDF does, and the book in stage 19 builds its index from them.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;resourceid appname="…" id="…"/&gt;</code> is an identifier for another system, here a help system that will link to this topic. <code>&lt;data name="…" value="…"/&gt;</code> is a named property with no defined meaning; a processor or a house rule can use it.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>See what reaches the page</strong> Save the file. Choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Build Deliverables...</strong>, and click <strong>Build All</strong>. Open <code>out/full/topics/what-is-audacity.html</code> and look at its <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. Before the prolog, it had only a <code>&lt;meta name="description"&gt;</code> from the short description and a default copyright with no owner. Now it also carries <code>&lt;meta name="keywords" content="Audacity, audio editor, open source"&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;meta name="rights" content="© 2026 DogsBay Ltd."&gt;</code>, which replaces the default.
      <p>That is all that reaches the head in the <code>html5</code> transform. The author, the dates, the audience, the category and the index terms stay in the source, for other outputs, for searches across the project, and for the policy in step 5.</p>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-2-the-prologs-of-the-other-topics" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 2: The prologs of the other topics<a href="#step-2-the-prologs-of-the-other-topics" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><code>topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita</code> A revision date, and three index-term forms.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n+++ b/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n@@ -4,6 +4,31 @@\n <concept id=\"what-is-digital-audio\">\n   <title>What is digital audio?</title>\n   <shortdesc>Digital audio is sound stored as numbers: samples taken thousands of times a second at a chosen precision.</shortdesc>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-10\"/>\n+      <revised modified=\"2026-03-02\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <audience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"novice\"/>\n+      <category>concept</category>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>digital audio</keyword>\n+        <keyword>sample rate</keyword>\n+        <keyword>bit depth</keyword>\n+        <keyword>waveform</keyword>\n+        <keyword>decibel</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>digital audio</indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>sample rate</indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>bit depth</indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>waveform</indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>decibel<index-see-also>amplitude</index-see-also></indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>amplitude</indexterm>\n+        <indexterm><index-sort-as>clipping</index-sort-as>Clipping (distortion)</indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n   <conbody>\n     <p>Digital audio is sound that has been converted into a numerical representation.\n     A few key concepts help you make better recordings and edits."} lang="diff" title="topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><code>topics/installing-audacity.dita</code> A nested index term.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/installing-audacity.dita\n+++ b/topics/installing-audacity.dita\n@@ -4,6 +4,25 @@\n <task id=\"installing-audacity\">\n   <title>Installing <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/></title>\n   <shortdesc>Download <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> from the official website, or install it with your package manager, and launch it once to finish setup.</shortdesc>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-12\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <audience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"novice\"/>\n+      <category>installation</category>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>install</keyword>\n+        <keyword>download</keyword>\n+        <keyword>Windows</keyword>\n+        <keyword>macOS</keyword>\n+        <keyword>Linux</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>installing<indexterm>Windows</indexterm><indexterm>macOS</indexterm><indexterm>Linux</indexterm></indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>download</indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n   <taskbody>\n     <prereq>\n       <p>You need about 250 MB of free disk space and an account that can install software.</p>"} lang="diff" title="topics/installing-audacity.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><code>topics/supported-audio-formats.dita</code> A revision date and an index redirect.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n+++ b/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n@@ -4,6 +4,26 @@\n <reference id=\"supported-audio-formats\">\n   <title>Supported audio formats</title>\n   <shortdesc>The audio formats <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> imports and exports, and when to use each one.</shortdesc>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-22\"/>\n+      <revised modified=\"2026-02-14\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <audience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"novice\"/>\n+      <category>reference</category>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>audio formats</keyword>\n+        <keyword>lossless</keyword>\n+        <keyword>lossy</keyword>\n+        <keyword>MP3</keyword>\n+        <keyword>FLAC</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>audio formats<indexterm>lossless</indexterm><indexterm>lossy</indexterm></indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>codecs<index-see>audio formats</index-see></indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n   <refbody>\n     <section>\n       <p><keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> imports and exports audio in several formats.\n@@ -46,7 +66,9 @@\n           </row>\n           <row>\n             <entry>MP3</entry>\n-            <entry><term keyref=\"gl-compression\">Compressed</term></entry>\n+            <entry>\n+              <term keyref=\"gl-compression\">Compressed</term>\n+            </entry>\n             <entry>Lossy</entry>\n             <entry>Widely supported, small files.\n             Needs the <i>LAME</i> encoder, bundled since version 2.3.2."} lang="diff" title="topics/supported-audio-formats.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the index terms</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;indexterm&gt;</code> is an entry in the index. An <code>&lt;indexterm&gt;</code> inside another is a sub-entry: <code>installing</code> with <code>Windows</code>, <code>macOS</code> and <code>Linux</code> under it.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;index-see&gt;</code> inside an entry redirects it: "codecs, <em>see</em> audio formats". The entry with <code>&lt;index-see&gt;</code> has no page of its own.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;index-see-also&gt;</code> adds a cross-reference under an entry that does have pages: "decibel … <em>see also</em> amplitude".</li>
      <li><code>&lt;index-sort-as&gt;</code> gives the text to sort by when it differs from the text shown: <code>Clipping (distortion)</code> sorts as <code>clipping</code>.</li>
      <li>The HTML pages do not show index entries, but you can list them. In the <strong>Terminal</strong> panel, run <code>dogsbay-xml index-audit .</code>. Each line is an index entry with the number of topics that carry it, and <code>&gt;</code> separates an entry from its sub-entry, for example <code>installing &gt; Windows</code>. A PDF index is built from the same terms.</li>
      <li><code>project-health</code> checks the index. Change the redirect to <code>audio format</code> and it reports, under <em>Index redirects to nothing</em>, <code>"codecs" see "audio format"</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit the remaining five tasks</strong> Same shape: author, <code>&lt;created&gt;</code>, audience, category, keywords and index terms. The noise task is the one topic for an intermediate audience.
      <CollapsibleAlert variant="note" title="topics/exporting-audio.dita" class="my-4">
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/exporting-audio.dita\n+++ b/topics/exporting-audio.dita\n@@ -4,6 +4,25 @@\n <task id=\"exporting-audio\">\n   <title>Exporting audio</title>\n   <shortdesc>Export a finished project as a WAV, MP3, OGG or FLAC file that other programs and devices can play.</shortdesc>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-22\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <audience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"novice\"/>\n+      <category>exporting</category>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>export</keyword>\n+        <keyword>MP3</keyword>\n+        <keyword>WAV</keyword>\n+        <keyword>FLAC</keyword>\n+        <keyword>OGG</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>exporting</indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>audio formats<indexterm>MP3</indexterm><indexterm>WAV</indexterm></indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n   <taskbody>\n     <prereq>\n       <p>Finish editing and save the project."} lang="diff" title="topics/exporting-audio.dita" />
      </CollapsibleAlert>
      <CollapsibleAlert variant="note" title="topics/preparing-to-record.dita" class="my-4">
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/preparing-to-record.dita\n+++ b/topics/preparing-to-record.dita\n@@ -4,6 +4,24 @@\n <task id=\"preparing-to-record\">\n   <title>Preparing to record</title>\n   <shortdesc>Checks to make in any order before you press Record: input level, sample rate and disk space.</shortdesc>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-15\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <audience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"novice\"/>\n+      <category>recording</category>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>recording</keyword>\n+        <keyword>levels</keyword>\n+        <keyword>sample rate</keyword>\n+        <keyword>disk space</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>recording<indexterm>preparing</indexterm><indexterm>levels</indexterm></indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>clipping</indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n   <taskbody>\n     <context>\n       <p>None of these checks depends on another, so do them in whatever order suits you.</p>"} lang="diff" title="topics/preparing-to-record.dita" />
      </CollapsibleAlert>
      <CollapsibleAlert variant="note" title="topics/recording-your-first-track.dita" class="my-4">
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\n+++ b/topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\n@@ -4,6 +4,24 @@\n <task id=\"recording-your-first-track\">\n   <title>Recording your first track</title>\n   <shortdesc>Make your first recording in <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/>: pick a microphone, record, stop and play it back.</shortdesc>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-15\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <audience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"novice\"/>\n+      <category>recording</category>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>recording</keyword>\n+        <keyword>microphone</keyword>\n+        <keyword>Device Toolbar</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>recording<indexterm>first track</indexterm></indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>microphone</indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>Device Toolbar</indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n   <taskbody>\n     <context>\n       <p>This task walks you through making your first audio recording."} lang="diff" title="topics/recording-your-first-track.dita" />
      </CollapsibleAlert>
      <CollapsibleAlert variant="note" title="topics/removing-background-noise.dita" class="my-4">
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/removing-background-noise.dita\n+++ b/topics/removing-background-noise.dita\n@@ -4,6 +4,24 @@\n <task id=\"removing-background-noise\">\n   <title>Removing background noise</title>\n   <shortdesc>Take a noise profile from a quiet section, then apply Noise Reduction to the whole track to remove hum, hiss and fan noise.</shortdesc>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-20\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <audience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"intermediate\"/>\n+      <category>editing</category>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>noise reduction</keyword>\n+        <keyword>noise profile</keyword>\n+        <keyword>effects</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>noise reduction</indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>effects<indexterm>Noise Reduction</indexterm></indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>hum<index-see>noise reduction</index-see></indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n   <taskbody>\n     <context>\n       <p>Background noise such as hum from electronics, hiss from a microphone or fan noise degrades a recording."} lang="diff" title="topics/removing-background-noise.dita" />
      </CollapsibleAlert>
      <CollapsibleAlert variant="note" title="topics/trimming-audio.dita" class="my-4">
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/trimming-audio.dita\n+++ b/topics/trimming-audio.dita\n@@ -4,6 +4,25 @@\n <task id=\"trimming-audio\">\n   <title>Trimming audio</title>\n   <shortdesc>Remove silence, false starts and mistakes by selecting a region of the waveform and deleting it.</shortdesc>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-18\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <audience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"novice\"/>\n+      <category>editing</category>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>trimming</keyword>\n+        <keyword>editing</keyword>\n+        <keyword>delete</keyword>\n+        <keyword>selection</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>trimming</indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>editing<indexterm>trimming</indexterm></indexterm>\n+        <indexterm>selection</indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n   <taskbody>\n     <context>\n       <p>Trimming removes unwanted audio from the start, end or middle of a track."} lang="diff" title="topics/trimming-audio.dita" />
      </CollapsibleAlert>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-3-the-glossary-and-the-shared-topics" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 3: The glossary and the shared topics<a href="#step-3-the-glossary-and-the-shared-topics" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><code>topics/glossary/audio-units.dita</code> The group gets a prolog of its own, and so does each entry inside it.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/glossary/audio-units.dita\n+++ b/topics/glossary/audio-units.dita\n@@ -3,9 +3,28 @@\n \n <glossgroup id=\"audio-units\">\n   <title>Units</title>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>units</keyword>\n+        <keyword>glossary</keyword>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n   <glossentry id=\"gl-decibel\">\n     <glossterm>decibel</glossterm>\n     <glossdef>A logarithmic unit for the ratio between two levels. In audio editing, 0 dB is the loudest level a file can hold, and quieter sounds are negative.</glossdef>\n+    <prolog>\n+      <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+      <metadata>\n+        <keywords>\n+          <keyword>decibel</keyword>\n+          <keyword>glossary</keyword>\n+          <indexterm>decibel</indexterm>\n+        </keywords>\n+      </metadata>\n+    </prolog>\n     <glossBody>\n       <glossSurfaceForm>decibel (dB)</glossSurfaceForm>\n       <glossAlt>\n@@ -16,6 +35,16 @@\n   <glossentry id=\"gl-hertz\">\n     <glossterm>hertz</glossterm>\n     <glossdef>Cycles per second. Sample rates are given in hertz: 44,100 Hz means 44,100 samples every second.</glossdef>\n+    <prolog>\n+      <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+      <metadata>\n+        <keywords>\n+          <keyword>hertz</keyword>\n+          <keyword>glossary</keyword>\n+          <indexterm>hertz</indexterm>\n+        </keywords>\n+      </metadata>\n+    </prolog>\n     <glossBody>\n       <glossSurfaceForm>hertz (Hz)</glossSurfaceForm>\n       <glossAlt>"} lang="diff" title="topics/glossary/audio-units.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read why the group has one</strong> A <code>&lt;glossgroup&gt;</code> is a topic, and the policy in step 5 says every topic needs a keyword. Give the entries prologs and leave the group without, and <code>project-health</code> reports <code>topics/glossary/audio-units.dita — [error] missing required &lt;keyword&gt;</code>. In a <code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> the <code>&lt;prolog&gt;</code> follows <code>&lt;glossdef&gt;</code>, and in a <code>&lt;glossgroup&gt;</code> it follows <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, before the first entry.
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit the six entry files</strong> Author and keywords, with the term as an index entry. No <code>&lt;created&gt;</code>: the policy requires a date on tasks only.
      <CollapsibleAlert variant="note" title="topics/glossary/g-bit-depth.dita, and the other five alike" class="my-4">
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/glossary/g-bit-depth.dita\n+++ b/topics/glossary/g-bit-depth.dita\n@@ -4,4 +4,14 @@\n <glossentry id=\"gl-bit-depth\">\n   <glossterm>Bit depth</glossterm>\n   <glossdef>The number of bits used to store each sample, which sets how precisely amplitude is recorded. 16-bit audio has 65,536 possible values; 24-bit has over 16 million.</glossdef>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>Bit depth</keyword>\n+        <keyword>glossary</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>Bit depth</indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n </glossentry>"} lang="diff" title="topics/glossary/g-bit-depth.dita" />
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/glossary/g-clipping.dita\n+++ b/topics/glossary/g-clipping.dita\n@@ -4,4 +4,14 @@\n <glossentry id=\"gl-clipping\">\n   <glossterm>Clipping</glossterm>\n   <glossdef>Distortion that occurs when a signal exceeds the maximum amplitude the system can represent. Clipped peaks are cut flat and cannot be repaired.</glossdef>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>Clipping</keyword>\n+        <keyword>glossary</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>Clipping</indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n </glossentry>"} lang="diff" title="topics/glossary/g-clipping.dita" />
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/glossary/g-compression.dita\n+++ b/topics/glossary/g-compression.dita\n@@ -4,4 +4,14 @@\n <glossentry id=\"gl-compression\">\n   <glossterm>Compression</glossterm>\n   <glossdef>Reducing the dynamic range of audio so that quiet parts are louder and loud parts quieter. Not to be confused with file compression such as MP3.</glossdef>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>Compression</keyword>\n+        <keyword>glossary</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>Compression</indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n </glossentry>"} lang="diff" title="topics/glossary/g-compression.dita" />
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/glossary/g-normalization.dita\n+++ b/topics/glossary/g-normalization.dita\n@@ -4,4 +4,14 @@\n <glossentry id=\"gl-normalization\">\n   <glossterm>Normalization</glossterm>\n   <glossdef>Raising or lowering the level of a recording so that its loudest peak sits at a chosen target, giving consistent loudness across clips.</glossdef>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>Normalization</keyword>\n+        <keyword>glossary</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>Normalization</indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n </glossentry>"} lang="diff" title="topics/glossary/g-normalization.dita" />
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/glossary/g-sample-rate.dita\n+++ b/topics/glossary/g-sample-rate.dita\n@@ -4,4 +4,14 @@\n <glossentry id=\"gl-sample-rate\">\n   <glossterm>Sample rate</glossterm>\n   <glossdef>The number of audio samples captured per second, measured in hertz. Higher sample rates capture more detail. CD audio uses 44,100 Hz; video production typically uses 48,000 Hz.</glossdef>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>Sample rate</keyword>\n+        <keyword>glossary</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>Sample rate</indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n </glossentry>"} lang="diff" title="topics/glossary/g-sample-rate.dita" />
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/glossary/g-waveform.dita\n+++ b/topics/glossary/g-waveform.dita\n@@ -4,4 +4,14 @@\n <glossentry id=\"gl-waveform\">\n   <glossterm>Waveform</glossterm>\n   <glossdef>The shape of a sound wave over time, drawn as amplitude against time. Audacity shows each track as a waveform.</glossdef>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>Waveform</keyword>\n+        <keyword>glossary</keyword>\n+        <indexterm>Waveform</indexterm>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n </glossentry>"} lang="diff" title="topics/glossary/g-waveform.dita" />
      </CollapsibleAlert>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit the two shared topics</strong> Shared topics are topics too. Their audience is <code>author</code>, because a writer is the only person who reads them, and their category is <code>warehouse</code>.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/shared/common-notes.dita\n+++ b/shared/common-notes.dita\n@@ -4,6 +4,21 @@\n <topic id=\"common-notes\">\n   <title>Common notes</title>\n   <shortdesc>Reusable notes, tips and lists that other topics pull in by conref. This topic is a warehouse and is not published on its own.</shortdesc>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-15\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <audience type=\"author\"/>\n+      <category>warehouse</category>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>reuse</keyword>\n+        <keyword>conref</keyword>\n+        <keyword>notes</keyword>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n   <body>\n     <note id=\"backup-warning\" type=\"warning\">This effect changes the audio data.\n     Save the project first; you can undo with <uicontrol>Ctrl+Z</uicontrol> (Windows and Linux) or <uicontrol>Cmd+Z</uicontrol> (macOS).</note>"} lang="diff" title="shared/common-notes.dita" />
      <CollapsibleAlert variant="note" title="shared/common-steps.dita" class="my-4">
        <CodeBlock code={"--- a/shared/common-steps.dita\n+++ b/shared/common-steps.dita\n@@ -4,6 +4,21 @@\n <task id=\"common-steps\">\n   <title>Common steps</title>\n   <shortdesc>Reusable steps that other tasks pull in by conref. This task is a warehouse and is not published on its own.</shortdesc>\n+  <prolog>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-15\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <metadata>\n+      <audience type=\"author\"/>\n+      <category>warehouse</category>\n+      <keywords>\n+        <keyword>reuse</keyword>\n+        <keyword>conref</keyword>\n+        <keyword>steps</keyword>\n+      </keywords>\n+    </metadata>\n+  </prolog>\n   <taskbody>\n     <steps>\n       <step id=\"select-region\">"} lang="diff" title="shared/common-steps.dita" />
      </CollapsibleAlert>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-4-metadata-for-the-publication" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 4: Metadata for the publication<a href="#step-4-metadata-for-the-publication" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><code>audacity-guide.ditamap</code>
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/audacity-guide.ditamap\n+++ b/audacity-guide.ditamap\n@@ -5,6 +5,20 @@\n   <title>Audacity User Guide</title>\n   <topicmeta>\n     <shortdesc>Record, edit and export audio with Audacity, from your first track to a finished file.</shortdesc>\n+    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n+    <publisher>DogsBay Ltd.</publisher>\n+    <copyright>\n+      <copyryear year=\"2026\"/>\n+      <copyrholder>DogsBay Ltd.</copyrholder>\n+    </copyright>\n+    <critdates>\n+      <created date=\"2026-01-10\"/>\n+    </critdates>\n+    <audience type=\"user\"/>\n+    <keywords>\n+      <keyword>Audacity</keyword>\n+      <keyword>audio editing</keyword>\n+    </keywords>\n   </topicmeta>\n \n   <mapref href=\"keydefs-product.ditamap\"/>"} lang="diff" title="audacity-guide.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the map metadata</strong> The map's <code>&lt;topicmeta&gt;</code> takes the same elements as a prolog, in the same order, without the <code>&lt;metadata&gt;</code> wrapper: <code>&lt;author&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;publisher&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;copyright&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;critdates&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;audience&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;keywords&gt;</code> sit directly inside it. Map metadata applies to the publication and, for the <code>html5</code> transform, cascades into every page: the <code>&lt;meta name="rights"&gt;</code> tag on each page comes from this copyright.
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>See the publication metadata</strong> Save the map, choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Build Deliverables...</strong>, and click <strong>Build All</strong>. The start page, <code>out/full/index.html</code>, carries the map's keywords in <code>&lt;meta name="keywords"&gt;</code> and a <code>&lt;meta name="rights"&gt;</code> tag from the map's copyright. A topic page such as <code>out/full/topics/what-is-digital-audio.html</code> also has the rights tag, although its topic has no copyright of its own. Its keywords stay the topic's own.
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-5-the-metadata-policy-and-the-check" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 5: The metadata policy and the check<a href="#step-5-the-metadata-policy-and-the-check" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Set the metadata policy</strong> The policy is kept with the project, in <code>.dogsbay/config.xml</code>, and makes fields required or recommended. Choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Metadata</strong> &gt; <strong>Edit Policy...</strong>. The dialog shows a table of rules, one rule per row. Add three rules:
      <Table class="my-4">
      <TableHeader>
      <TableRow>
      <TableHead>Topic type (blank = any)</TableHead>
      <TableHead>Field</TableHead>
      <TableHead>Presence</TableHead>
      <TableHead>Allowed values (space-separated)</TableHead>
      <TableHead>Pattern</TableHead>
      </TableRow>
      </TableHeader>
      <TableBody>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>task</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>created</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>required</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>(empty)</TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>\d{"{"}4{"}"}-\d\d-\d\d</code></TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell>(empty)</TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>author</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>recommended</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>(empty)</TableCell>
      <TableCell>(empty)</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell>(empty)</TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>keyword</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>required</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>(empty)</TableCell>
      <TableCell>(empty)</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      </TableBody>
      </Table>
      <p>For each rule, click <strong>Add rule</strong>. A new row starts with the field <code>author</code> and the presence <code>required</code>. Choose the <strong>Field</strong> and the <strong>Presence</strong> from their lists, and double-click a text cell to type in it. When the table shows the three rules, click <strong>OK</strong>. A message confirms that the rules were saved to <code>.dogsbay/config.xml</code>; click <strong>OK</strong>.</p>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the rules</strong>
      <ul>
      <li>Each rule names a field, a presence and, optionally, a topic type and a pattern. A missing <code>required</code> field is an error and fails the check; a missing <code>recommended</code> field is a warning, shown beside a file's errors.</li>
      <li><code>created</code> is required on every task, and must match <code>\d{"{"}4{"}"}-\d\d-\d\d</code>. Concepts, references and glossary entries may leave it out.</li>
      <li><code>author</code> is recommended everywhere. <code>keyword</code> is required everywhere, which is the rule that reaches the glossary group.</li>
      <li><code>project-health</code> applies the policy as one of its checks, so the health stage of <strong>Check Project</strong> and <code>dogsbay-xml check</code> enforces it. The editor applies the same policy. DITA-OT and other DITA tools do not read this policy.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Format and check your work</strong>
      <p>Format the topics: choose <strong>XML</strong> &gt; <strong>Format</strong> in each changed topic and save it, or choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Project Tools</strong> &gt; <strong>Format Project</strong> to format every file at once. From the command line, run:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita topics/glossary/*.dita shared/*.dita"} lang="bash" />
      <p>Then check the project. In the editor, choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Check Project</strong> and read the result in the <strong>Project Validation</strong> panel. From the command line, run:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"dogsbay-xml check ."} lang="bash" />
      <p>Example output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"health   clean, with warnings\n  unused key: start-here  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:26] — nothing references it\n  unused key: digital-audio  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:33] — nothing references it\n  unused key: gl-normalization  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/keydefs-glossary.ditamap:10] — nothing references it\nbuild    full                 ok  /home/you/my-audacity-guide/out/full\noutput   clean (full)\nReady: the project is healthy, every deliverable built, and the output of full holds together. 3 unused keys above: worth knowing, and not treated as failures."} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>The unused keys are defined for later stages: stage 14 refers to <code>gl-normalization</code>, and stage 18 uses <code>start-here</code> and <code>digital-audio</code>.</p>
      <p>The check also builds the guide into <code>out/full/</code>. The <code>html5</code> pages carry the keywords and rights tags from step 1 and step 4. The PDF transform uses the index terms to generate an index.</p>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <p>Test the metadata policy. In <em>Trimming audio</em>, put the cursor in <code>&lt;critdates&gt;</code>, choose <strong>XML</strong> &gt; <strong>Select Element</strong>, delete the element, save, and check your work. The check stops at health and names the file. Example output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"health   NOT CLEAN\n  /home/you/my-audacity-guide/topics/trimming-audio.dita:7  created — missing required <created @date>\n  (run project-health for the full report)\n  unused key: start-here  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:26] — nothing references it\n  unused key: digital-audio  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:33] — nothing references it\n  unused key: gl-normalization  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/keydefs-glossary.ditamap:10] — nothing references it\nNot ready: the project itself has faults. The build and the built output were not checked."} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>The <strong>Project Validation</strong> panel, or <code>dogsbay-xml project-health .</code>, gives the full report:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"Root map: audacity-guide.ditamap (project config); house rules: none (none configured)\nMetadata policy (1 of 21 files):\n  /home/you/my-audacity-guide/topics/trimming-audio.dita — [error] missing required <created @date>\nUnused keys (3):\n  start-here  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:26]\n  digital-audio  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:33]\n  gl-normalization  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/keydefs-glossary.ditamap:10]\n\nSummary\n  Metadata policy               1  in 1 of 21 files (1 error(s), 0 warning(s))\n    missing required <created @date>                                  1\n  Unused keys                   3"} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>The file is valid; <code>&lt;critdates&gt;</code> is optional to the DTD. The DogsBay XML policy, not the DITA DTD, makes it required for tasks in this project.</p>
      <p>Undo the policy test with <strong>Edit</strong> &gt; <strong>Undo</strong>, save, and check again. Then test the element order. Move <code>&lt;resourceid&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;data&gt;</code> in <em>What is Audacity?</em> to before <code>&lt;metadata&gt;</code>, and check your work. The file is no longer valid, and the check names it with the line, column, and message. Example output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"health   NOT CLEAN\n  invalid: /home/you/my-audacity-guide/topics/what-is-audacity.dita\n    29:12  The content of element type \"prolog\" does not match its content model.\n  (run project-health for the full report)\n  unused key: start-here  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:26] — nothing references it\n  unused key: digital-audio  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:33] — nothing references it\n  unused key: gl-normalization  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/keydefs-glossary.ditamap:10] — nothing references it\nNot ready: the project itself has faults. The build and the built output were not checked."} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>The <strong>Project Validation</strong> panel, or <code>dogsbay-xml project-health .</code>, gives the full report. It begins:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"Invalid files (1 of 21):\n  /home/you/my-audacity-guide/topics/what-is-audacity.dita:\n    29:12  error: The content of element type \"prolog\" does not match its content model."} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>Undo the change and check again. Confirm that the check reports <code>Ready</code> before you continue.</p>
      <h2 id="what-you-learned" class="group scroll-mt-20">What you learned<a href="#what-you-learned" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;prolog&gt;</code> after <code>&lt;shortdesc&gt;</code>, with <code>&lt;author&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;copyright&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;critdates&gt;</code> (<code>&lt;created&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;revised&gt;</code>), <code>&lt;metadata&gt;</code> (<code>&lt;audience&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;category&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;keywords&gt;</code>), then <code>&lt;resourceid&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;data&gt;</code>, in that order.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;indexterm&gt;</code>, nested for sub-entries; <code>&lt;index-see&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;index-see-also&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;index-sort-as&gt;</code> inside an entry.</li>
      <li>A <code>&lt;glossgroup&gt;</code> and a shared topic are topics and carry prologs like any other.</li>
      <li>Map <code>&lt;topicmeta&gt;</code> takes the same elements for the publication.</li>
      <li>A metadata policy, set in <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Metadata</strong> &gt; <strong>Edit Policy...</strong>: rules by topic type, field, presence and pattern, enforced by <code>project-health</code> and so by the check.</li>
      <li>End of Part 2: a map with structure and a reltable, a key space, a shared topic, a glossary and metadata, published as one HTML5 deliverable.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="next-lesson" class="group scroll-mt-20">Next lesson<a href="#next-lesson" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
